        "question": {
          ":question/id": "a2460ddb-9851-5db5-a9f1-896353da46a8",
          ":question/problem": "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ for $f(x,y)=x^{2} + x y^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=x,\\quad r=x^{2}\\\\\nu&=y^{2},\\quad v=\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_y&=2 y\\\\\nv_y&=\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\\nr_y&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\\\\n&=x\\left[\\left(2 y\\right)\\left(\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)+\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\right]+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=x \\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 y \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
