          ":question/problem": "For $f(x,y)=x y \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$, evaluate $f_y(2,\\pi)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=x,\\quad r=- \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\\nu&=y,\\quad v=\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_y&=1\\\\\nv_y&=- \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\\nr_y&=- \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\\\\n&=x\\left[\\left(1\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)+\\left(y\\right)\\left(- \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\right]+\\left(- \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=x \\left(- y \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right) - \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=2$ and $y=\\pi$ and combine the exact values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(2,\\pi)&=2\\left[\\left(1\\right)\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(\\pi\\right)\\left(0\\right)\\right]+\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
